## ea-geo | Surprising geography | earth | 0 | - | ea-sky
quiz 3 | Which country has the most time zones (including overseas territories)?
* France
o Russia
o USA
o China
= France spans 12 time zones thanks to its overseas territories.
= China uses a single time zone despite being about 5,000 km wide.
> Africa is bigger than you think

explain 3 | The Mercator map shrinks Africa
- Mercator projection stretches areas near the poles.
- Greenland looks similar in size to Africa. Africa is about 14 times larger.
- *Africa could hold the USA, China, India and most of Europe.*
> Canada's coastline

quiz 3 | Which country has the longest coastline?
* Canada
o Russia
o Australia
o Norway
= About 200,000 km including its many islands.
= Coastline length depends on how finely you measure: the "coastline paradox".
> The coastline paradox

explain 4 | The coastline paradox
- Measure Britain's coast with a 100 km ruler, then a 10 km ruler: the second gives a longer answer.
- Smaller rulers capture more wiggles. Coastlines are fractal-like.
- *Benoit Mandelbrot's 1967 paper asked: How long is the coast of Britain?*
> Lake Baikal

explain 3 | Lake Baikal holds about 20% of Earth's unfrozen surface fresh water
- It's over 1,600 m deep and about 25 million years old.
- It holds more water than all five North American Great Lakes combined.
- *It's home to the nerpa, one of the only freshwater seals.*
> Next: languages of the world 2

## la-ling2 | Language curiosities | lang | 0 | - | la-ling
explain 3 | Some languages have no words for left and right
- Guugu Yimithirr speakers in Australia describe positions with cardinal directions: "the cup is north of your plate".
- Speakers maintain a constant sense of direction.
- *Even in dark rooms, they'd point accurately north.*
> Pirahã and counting

explain 4 | A language without exact numbers
- The Pirahã language of the Amazon seems to lack words for exact quantities, with terms like "few" and "many".
- In experiments, speakers struggled to match exact quantities above three.
- *The finding is debated, but it raises questions about language and thought.*
> Whistled languages

story 4 | Silbo Gomero: a whistled language
- On La Gomera in the Canary Islands, people whistle Spanish across ravines up to several kilometres.
- It's taught in schools and listed by UNESCO as intangible cultural heritage.
- *Whistled languages exist in Turkey, Mexico, France and elsewhere.*
> English's biggest borrowing

quiz 3 | Roughly what proportion of English vocabulary comes from French and Latin combined?
* Around 50–60%
o About 5%
o About 90%
o About 25%
= After the Norman Conquest (1066), French flooded into English, especially law, food and government.
= Everyday core words (the, is, water, house) are mostly Germanic.
> Next: world cultures

## wo-cult | Cultures and customs | history | 0 | - | hi-inv
story 4 | Kintsugi: repairing pottery with gold
- In Japan, broken ceramics are mended with lacquer mixed with gold powder.
- The cracks are highlighted rather than hidden.
- *It's often read as embracing imperfection and history.*
> The Olympics once had art medals

story 4 | Olympic gold medals for painting
- From 1912 to 1948, the Olympics awarded medals for architecture, literature, music, painting and sculpture.
- Pierre de Coubertin, founder of the modern Games, won a literature gold in 1912 under a pseudonym.
- *They were dropped because artists were professionals.*
> Days of the week

quiz 3 | Wednesday is named after which god?
* Woden (Odin)
o Mercury
o Thor
o Saturn
= Woden's day. Thursday is Thor's day; Saturday is Saturn's.
= In French, mercredi is Mercury's day: the Romans matched Mercury with Woden.
> Calendars

explain 3 | Why February has 28 days
- The early Roman calendar had ten months and an uncounted winter gap.
- January and February were added later; February ended up shortest.
- *September to December mean seventh to tenth months, from when March started the year.*
> Next: Earth science 2

## ea-earth2 | Earth science 2 | earth | 0 | - | ea-geo
explain 4 | Earth's magnetic field flips
- The magnetic poles have reversed many times, recorded in volcanic rocks on the seafloor.
- The last full reversal was about 780,000 years ago.
- *During a flip the field weakens, but there's no evidence of mass extinctions tied to reversals.*
> Supervolcanoes

explain 4 | Yellowstone sits on a supervolcano
- Its last huge eruption, about 640,000 years ago, spread ash over much of North America.
- Scientists monitor it closely; the yearly chance of another super-eruption is very small.
- *Volcanic winters from smaller eruptions, like Tambora in 1815, have caused famines.*
> Earthquakes

quiz 3 | Each step up on the moment magnitude scale releases roughly how much more energy?
* About 32 times
o 10 times
o 2 times
o 100 times
= Two steps up is about 1,000 times the energy.
> The ozone layer recovered

story 4 | The ozone hole is healing
- 1985: British scientists reported a huge seasonal ozone hole over Antarctica, caused by CFCs.
- The 1987 Montreal Protocol phased CFCs out worldwide.
- *The ozone layer is projected to recover to 1980 levels around the middle of this century.* A global environmental treaty that worked.
